{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And now, please, let the strength of YHWH be magnified, as You spoke, saying</b> — and what is that spoken word? <b>YHWH, slow to anger</b>, toward the righteous and toward the wicked. When Moses ascended on high, he found the Holy One, blessed be He, writing \"YHWH, slow to anger.\" He said to Him: \"Toward the righteous?\" He said to him: \"Also toward the wicked.\" He said to Him: \"Let the wicked perish!\" He said to him: \"By your life, you will have need of this thing.\" When they sinned with the calf and with the spies and he prayed before Him with slowness of anger, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: \"But did you not tell Me, toward the righteous?\" He said to Him: \"But did You not tell me, also toward the wicked — let the strength of YHWH be magnified to fulfill Your word.\" This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 14:18) from aggadic teachings (Sanhedrin 111a). And by the way of truth, you will recognize it because the Name in this place is written with aleph-dalet (i.e., Adonai), and it says that the magnification should be through the power of mercy, for the attribute of judgment is stretched out against them. And in the attributes he mentioned <b>slow to anger and abundant in kindness</b>, but he did not mention \"truth,\" for by the attribute of truth they would be liable, and he did not mention \"preserving kindness to thousands,\" for Moses did not pray now by the merit of the fathers, and he did not mention at all in this prayer Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. And the reason is because the land was given to the fathers and from them they would inherit it, but they were rebelling against their fathers and did not desire their gift which the fathers had cherished greatly — so how could he say \"which You swore to them by You… and all this land I will give to your seed\" (Exodus 32:13), when they say, we cannot accept this gift? And he did not mention \"and sin\" because these are intentional and rebellious sinners. And I do not know why he did not mention \"compassionate and gracious\" — perhaps Moses knew that the judgment was stretched out over them and He would never forgive, therefore he requested only prolongation of anger, that He not kill them as one man and not slaughter them like sheep in the wilderness so that they die in a plague. And because he requested now only prolongation of anger, He said to him \"I have forgiven according to your word,\" that He would be for them slow to anger and abundant in kindness. And He mentioned <b>visiting the iniquity of fathers</b>, to say that if He sees fit not to wipe away their iniquity, He will visit the iniquity of fathers upon their children and take them with prolongation of anger, and from this came the decree to establish for them weeping for generations on this night, for He visited their iniquity upon their seed. And Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 14:19) said that because we find \"if they see the land\" after \"I have forgiven according to your word,\" we know that the word \"please forgive\" means prolongation of anger, and likewise \"and it shall be forgiven him\" — until he does complete repentance. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that selichah is the removal of punishment, according to the words of Onkelos, and the sacrifices remove the punishment of the inadvertent sinner from upon him, and likewise \"good and forgiving\" (Psalms 86:5). And He said \"I have forgiven according to your word\" to the people in general, that He would not strike them with plague and dispossess them and make of him a great and mighty nation instead of them while they and their seed perish from the earth — but He would forgive them in that their children would inherit the land, and that they themselves would not die in the plague; rather, He decreed that their carcasses would perish in the wilderness and every one would die there when his day came. Now, in Mishneh Torah Moses mentioned in the matter of the calf the prayer that he prayed on their behalf, and said \"And I fell down before HaShem as at the first\" (Deuteronomy 9:18), and there he explained (verse 26) his prayer: \"And I prayed to HaShem and said\" etc., and he also mentioned there his prayer on behalf of Aaron (verse 20), but he did not mention regarding the spies that he prayed on their behalf at all. And all this is for the reason I mentioned, for he did not pray to forgive them but rather to bear with them and prolong His anger and visit the iniquity of fathers upon sons, and his prayer on their behalf was not complete, and therefore he did not mention it to them, for they could have argued against him:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The phrase משנה תורה here refers to the book of Deuteronomy, not to Maimonides' Mishneh Torah; the reference is to Moses' retelling in Deuteronomy 9."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:05:50.782954639Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}